<section>
  <h2>4. Memory sizes (estimates)</h2>
  <p class=3D"note">Estimated from the model sizes (ModernBERT-large =E2=89=
=88 0.4 B parameters, DeBERTa-v3-large =E2=89=88 0.44 B, bf16 on the GPU)
  and <code>--batch-size 256</code>, <code>--max-length 512</code>. Not mea=
sured. Read the real numbers in the sizing run.</p>
  <div class=3D"tbl"><table>
    <tbody><tr><th></th><th><span class=3D"tag cpu">RAM</span></th><th><spa=
n class=3D"tag gpu">GPU memory</span></th></tr>
    <tr><td>One copy</td><td>=E2=89=88 3=E2=80=936 GB (Python, PyTorch, mod=
els while loading, chunk, buffers)</td><td>=E2=89=88 4=E2=80=938 GB (CUDA c=
ontext =E2=89=88 0.5=E2=80=931, models =E2=89=88 1.7, batch work =E2=89=88 =
2=E2=80=935)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>One GPU (2 copies)</td><td>=E2=80=93</td><td>=E2=89=88 8=E2=80=
=9316 GB of 141 GB</td></tr>
    <tr><td>One machine (16 copies)</td><td>=E2=89=88 50=E2=80=93100 GB</td=
><td>=E2=89=88 64=E2=80=93128 GB over 8 GPUs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5 machines (80 copies)</td><td>=E2=89=88 250=E2=80=93500 GB in =
total, never shared</td><td>=E2=89=88 320=E2=80=93640 GB over 40 GPUs</td><=
/tr>
    <tr><td>What raises it</td><td>more copies; very long lines</td><td>big=
ger <code>--batch-size</code>; more copies per GPU</td></tr>
    <tr><td>When it runs out</td><td>a copy is <b>Killed</b> (<code>dmesg |=
 tail</code>)</td><td><code>CUDA out of memory</code> in <code>logs/shard-k=
.log</code></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>5. Watching GPU, CPU and memory</h2>
  <div class=3D"two">
  <div>
    <h2>Ubuntu, 8 =C3=97 H200</h2>
    <p><b>GPU</b> (installed with the driver):</p>
<pre>nvidia-smi -l 5          # full table every 5 s
nvidia-smi dmon -s um    # per GPU: use % and memory, every second
nvidia-smi --query-gpu=3Dindex,utilization.gpu,memory.used,memory.total \
  --format=3Dcsv -l 5
nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps=3Dpid,used_memory --format=3Dcsv   # per co=
py</pre>
    <p><b>CPU and RAM</b> (always there):</p>
<pre>top                      # press 1: every core; M: sort by memory
free -h -s 5             # RAM every 5 s
vmstat 5                 # CPU, RAM, swap, waiting for disk
ps -C python -o pid,%cpu,rss,args --sort=3D-rss   # each copy's RAM (rss, K=
B)
dmesg | tail             # "Killed process" =3D out of RAM</pre>
    <p><b>Nicer screens</b> (need installing; on the offline server bring t=
he packages along):</p>
<pre>htop                     # apt: htop
nvtop                    # apt: nvtop, GPU + processes, like htop
iostat -x 5              # apt: sysstat, disk speed
pip install nvitop; nvitop   # GPU, CPU, RAM in one screen</pre>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h2>Windows 10, RTX 5090</h2>
    <p><b>GPU</b>: <code>nvidia-smi</code> comes with the driver and works =
in PowerShell the same way:</p>
<pre>nvidia-smi -l 5
nvidia-smi --query-gpu=3Dutilization.gpu,memory.used,memory.total `
  --format=3Dcsv -l 5</pre>
    <p><b>Task Manager</b> (Ctrl+Shift+Esc) =E2=86=92 <b>Performance</b> =
=E2=86=92 <b>GPU</b>. PyTorch work does not show in the
    default "3D" graph: click a graph title and choose <b>Cuda</b> or <b>Co=
mpute_0</b>. "Dedicated GPU memory" is the
    GPU memory (32 GB on a 5090). The <b>CPU</b> and <b>Memory</b> tabs sho=
w cores and RAM.</p>
    <p><b>PowerShell counters</b>:</p>
<pre>Get-Counter '\Processor(_Total)\% Processor Time' -Continuous
Get-Counter '\Memory\Available MBytes' -SampleInterval 5 -Continuous
Get-Process python | Sort-Object WS -Descending |
  Select-Object Id, CPU, @{n=3D'RAM GB';e=3D{[math]::Round($_.WS/1GB,1)}}</=
pre>
    <p><b>Resource Monitor</b> (<code>resmon</code>): CPU, memory and disk =
per process.</p>
    <p class=3D"note">A 5090 has 32 GB instead of 141 GB: start with 1=E2=
=80=932 copies and <code>--batch-size 128=E2=80=93256</code>.</p>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>
